            "text": "{\"strengths\":[\"The written explanation gives a complete causal chain: drought reduces harvests, supply decreases at every price, a shortage occurs at P1, and price rises to a new equilibrium.\",\"The diagram visibly shows unchanged demand and a leftward shift of supply, with the new intersection indicating higher price and lower quantity.\",\"Terminology and notation are used accurately and consistently, including S1/S2, P1/P2 and Q1/Q2.\"],\"improvements\":[],\"mistakes\":[],\"examinerComment\":\"Estimated practice mark: 4/4. Your explanation and visible diagram are fully consistent with the scenario. You correctly show that the drought shifts wheat supply left while demand is unchanged, leading to a higher equilibrium price and lower equilibrium quantity.\",\"studyNext\":\"Practise explaining other competitive-equilibrium shifts, especially demand increases/decreases and simultaneous shifts in demand and supply.\",\"assessmentFormat\":\"paper_2_c_to_f_diagram_and_explanation\",\"paper\":\"paper_2\",\"questionPart\":\"unknown\",\"levelRelevance\":\"shared_sl_hl\",\"assessmentSkills\":[\"diagram_explanation\",\"economic_analysis\",\"application\"],\"commandTerm\":\"explain\",\"commandTermLabel\":\"explain\",\"syllabusUnit\":\"unit_2\",\"syllabusTopic\":\"2.3\",\"topicLabel\":\"Competitive market equilibrium\",\"classificationConfidence\":\"high\",\"markingConfidence\":\"high\",\"diagramExpected\":true,\"diagramSubmitted\":true,\"diagramAssessmentStatus\":\"submitted_and_assessed\",\"workingsExpected\":false,\"workingsSubmitted\":false,\"workingsAssessmentStatus\":\"not_relevant\",\"attachmentContent\":\"diagram\",\"assessableEarned\":4,\"markBreakdown\":[{\"label\":\"Diagram\",\"awarded\":4,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"The visible diagram shows a downward-sloping demand curve, two supply curves with the new supply position to the left, and two demand-supply intersections showing higher price and lower quantity after the shift. The intended labels and equilibrium projections are also visible, although some are blurred.\"},{\"label\":\"Economic analysis\",\"awarded\":4,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"The answer accurately explains reduced harvests as a fall in supply at each price, a shortage at the initial equilibrium price, upward pressure on price, and a lower equilibrium quantity traded.\"},{\"label\":\"Application to context\",\"awarded\":4,\"available\":4,\"reason\":\"The explanation applies the drought, reduced wheat harvest and unchanged wheat demand directly to the market outcome.\"}],\"bandRationale\":null,\"limitations\":[],\"componentEvaluation\":{\"diagram\":2,\"explanation\":2,\"diagramReason\":\"A creditworthy, correctly oriented wheat-market diagram is visible: demand is unchanged and the supply curve shifts left, producing a higher-price, lower-quantity equilibrium.\",\"explanationReason\":\"The written response provides the complete mechanism from drought-reduced harvests to reduced supply, shortage at P1, a rise in price and a fall in quantity traded.\",\"incompatibleMechanisms\":false,\"mismatchEvidence\":\"\",\"labelingDeficiency\":false,\"labelingEvidence\":\"Some photographed labels are blurred, but the axes, demand curve, supply positions and P1/P2 and Q1/Q2 projections are sufficiently identifiable from the visible evidence.\",\"rootErrors\":[]}}"
